+# Host logs and crash reports
+
+Issue 23. A run of `horizon_run.py` keeps a log and writes a report when it crashes. Both go to the state folder, the one the Qt front end already uses for its own logs:
+
+```
+$XDG_STATE_HOME/nx2linux/logs        when XDG_STATE_HOME is an absolute path
+~/.local/state/nx2linux/logs         otherwise
+```
+
+The code is `engine/dm/tools/horizon_crash.py`. The folder is created with mode 0700 and the files with mode 0600.
+
+## What is written
+
+| File | What it holds | Safe to attach to an issue |
+|---|---|---|
+| `host-<title>-<UTC time>-<pid>.log` | Everything the run prints, on stdout and stderr. Your home folder is shortened to `~` | No, read it first. It is the raw local log and can hold strings the game prints |
+| `crash-<title>-<UTC time>-<pid>.json` | One report per crash, up to 5 for a run (`-2`, `-3`... after the first) | Yes, it is made for that |
+| `crash-<title>-<UTC time>-<pid>.raw` | What Python's faulthandler wrote when the process died of a signal | No, but the next run turns it into a `.json` report and removes it |
+
+The console does not change. The log is a copy.
+
+## The kinds of report
+
+| `kind` | When |
+|---|---|
+| `guest-fault` | A guest thread stopped on a fault, for example an access to an address no region covers |
+| `boot-fault` | The boot sequence (`nn::init::Start` and `nnMain`) faulted |
+| `guest-break` | The game called `svcBreak`, which is how a failed assertion ends. The host exits right after |
+| `python-exception` | The host itself raised and nobody caught it, in the main thread or in any other |
+| `fatal-signal` | The process died of SIGBUS, SIGILL, SIGABRT or SIGFPE, or of SIGSEGV before the first guest thread ran |
+
+## What a report holds
+
+- The schema version, the kind, the UTC time, the process id, the title id, the seconds since the run started.
+- The host: Python version, system, kernel, machine, CPU count, number of engine patches.
+- The fault text, the guest thread, and the call chain as `module+offset` (for example `main+0x1a2b3c`).
+- For each guest thread, its last 16 kernel calls with the address of the call and how long ago it was, and the count of every kernel call of the run.
+- The `NX2LINUX_*` and `AOT_*` variables in the environment, with the values redacted. `NX2LINUX_NICK` is left out.
+- The GPU name when the GPU renders.
+
+## What a report does not hold
+
+- No path of yours. The home folder becomes `~`, the folders given with `--modules`, `--romfs` and `--cache` become `<modules>`, `<romfs>` and `<cache>`, the engine folder becomes `<engine>`, any other `/home/<name>` becomes `~`.
+- No user name, no key file name (anything ending in `.keys`) and no package name (`.nsp`, `.xci`, `.nsz`, `.xcz`).
+- No memory of the game and no register values, unless you ask.
+- No name of a function of the game. The addresses are `module+offset`.
+
+Three switches add detail when you want it for yourself: `NX2LINUX_CRASH_REGS=1` adds x0 to x30, `NX2LINUX_CRASH_SYMBOLS=1` adds the nearest function name of the game, and `NX2LINUX_CRASH_FAULTHANDLER=0` turns off the signal handler (below). The two first make a report that is no longer free of game data, so read it before you share it.
+
+## Switches
+
+| Variable | Effect |
+|---|---|
+| `NX2LINUX_HOST_LOG=0` | No log and no report |
+| `NX2LINUX_LOG_KEEP=<n>` | How many host logs to keep, 20 by default. Only files named `host-*.log` are ever removed. Crash reports are kept up to 50 |
+| `NX2LINUX_CRASH_REGS=1` | Add the registers to a report |
+| `NX2LINUX_CRASH_SYMBOLS=1` | Add the function name of the game next to the address |
+| `NX2LINUX_CRASH_FAULTHANDLER=0` | Do not install the signal handler |
+
+## Limits
+
+- The AOT runtime installs its own SIGSEGV handler when a guest thread first runs. For a fault that is not a guest access it restores the default action. A SEGV inside a host library after that point ends the process with no raw dump, and the shell reports the exit status 139. The other fatal signals are caught. Chaining the runtime's handler to the previous one would close the gap. It changes `aot_runtime.cpp` and it is not known whether that forces a rebuild of the cache, so it is left out.
+- The report cannot say what the game was doing in its own terms. It gives addresses and the last kernel calls of each thread.
+- A host that is killed by SIGKILL, or that loses power, leaves nothing.
+- The state folder must be writable. If it is not, the run goes on and says so once, with no log.
